FILE: app/src/beacon.ts
```ts
import { addAnimationTask, createAnimationTask } from "@babylonjs/lite";
import type { AnimationManager } from "@babylonjs/lite";

const RISE_SECONDS = 0.1;
const FALL_SECONDS = 0.1;

export function startBeacon(manager: AnimationManager, lamp: { intensity: number }, periodSeconds: number): (strength: number) => void {
    let strength = 1;
    let phase = 0; // seconds into the current period

    const task = createAnimationTask((_manager, deltaMs) => {
        phase = (phase + deltaMs / 1000) % periodSeconds;

        let value = 0;
        if (phase < RISE_SECONDS) {
            value = phase / RISE_SECONDS;
        } else if (phase < RISE_SECONDS + FALL_SECONDS) {
            value = (RISE_SECONDS + FALL_SECONDS - phase) / FALL_SECONDS;
        }
        lamp.intensity = value * strength;
    });

    addAnimationTask(manager, task);

    return (newStrength: number) => {
        strength = newStrength;
    };
}
```

I wrote `src/beacon.ts` as a custom animation task on the manager. It keeps its own phase within the period, so changing the strength only scales later frames and never shifts the rhythm. I chose this over a keyframed property clip because the clip bakes the peak into its sample data, and the declarations give no safe way to change that mid-playback.

I couldn't run or type-check it. I assumed `createAnimationTask` and `addAnimationTask` are exported from the `@babylonjs/lite` root and that the manager calls an active task's update with `deltaMs` on every update. The first update advances the phase by that frame's delta before writing the intensity, and the lamp isn't written before then.
